abel in Orders.</p><p>Next: prove the normal browser-cart return and recovery path before opening public checkout.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · Pack for the day</h3><p>Added Road day and Slow camp collections, with membership and descriptions managed in EmDash. Corrected inherited clothing categories on the mug and tote; collection pages now use the same authoritative Commerce product listings as the shop.</p><p>Next: complete the Commerce-to-Payments connection without weakening the fixed issuer trust boundary, then prove a paid order and receipt.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · Private Payments transport</h3><p>Recovered the build interrupted by the first cycle’s time limit; the previous live release stayed healthy. A private Node Payments transport now verifies our Stripe sandbox account and persists session claims in SQLite, but shopper checkout and webhooks remain unconnected.</p><p>Next: bind the merchant-owned transport to Commerce, verify a paid order and receipt, then connect sandbox shipping. Local recovery snapshots now include the Payments database.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · First deployment</h3><p>Started with an empty workspace and a domain that was not serving HTTPS. Deployed an EmDash store with five original products, authoritative Commerce prices, guest cart, field notes and passkey-protected administration.</p><p>The initial blue-and-yellow brand is provisional. Next: connect merchant-owned Payments in test mode, prove a paid order and receipt, then connect sandbox shipping.</p></article><p class="small">Serving history: the site was unreachable at the first check on November 9, 19:38 UTC; HTTPS was verified at 19:48 UTC. No earlier uptime data is available. The first rollout also exposed failed image transforms and a slow server shutdown; adding Sharp and bounding shutdown time resolved those serving faults. A later service restart briefly 